# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Responding to Change — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Chapter Five — Responding to Change'
HEAD = '''Ten passages. The first four chapters argue; this one is a <b>manual of cases</b>. Marquis Wu puts nine bad situations one after another and Wu Qi gives an answer to each. The order of the bad situations is itself deliberate: first, your chariots are sound and your horses good and your commander brave and your soldiers strong, and you meet the enemy suddenly and the ranks come apart; then the enemy is many and you are few; then the enemy is many <i>and</i> strong, with his back to a great obstacle, mountain on one side and water on the other, deep ditches and high ramparts, and plenty of food besides; then he is close up against you and there is no way out; then you meet him in a ravine; then you are boxed in a valley with high mountains right and left; then you are in a great marsh with your wheels sunk and your shafts under water; then it has rained for days, the horses are bogged and the chariots stopped and you are taking fire from all sides; and last, raiders arrive out of nowhere to carry off the cattle in your fields. <b>From open battle to encirclement, from encirclement to the weather, and from the weather to plain robbery.</b>

The answers grow lighter as the questions grow smaller. The heaviest is the <b>five columns on five roads</b> of the third passage: against a strong enemy who sits behind his works, equip a thousand chariots and ten thousand horse, add the foot, divide into five columns, and send each by a different road. Five columns on five roads, and the enemy cannot tell which to guard. Then send spies to read his mind; if he will listen, raise the siege and go; if he will not, <b>behead his envoy and burn his letter</b> and attack on all five roads at once. The lightest is the last passage: when a town has been taken, do not fell its trees, tear down its houses, take its grain, kill its livestock or burn its stores — “to show the people that you have no cruelty in you” — and any who ask to surrender are to be accepted and settled. A chapter about change ends on a rule that <b>does not change: leave the inhabitants’ things alone.</b>

Two smaller things are worth noting. The second passage converts terrain into odds: “to strike ten with one, nothing is better than a defile; to strike a hundred with ten, nothing better than broken ground; to strike ten thousand with a thousand, nothing better than an obstacle.” And the fifth gives six words that admit there is no clever answer at all — in hill country, wooded valleys, deep mountains and great marshes, “go fast and get out; do not take your time.”'''

PARA = {
 'p5-1': 'Marquis Wu asked: “The chariots are sound, the horses good, the commander brave and the soldiers strong; then suddenly we meet the enemy, the ranks fall into confusion and the columns break. What then?” Wu Qi replied: “The rule of battle is this. By day the signals are the banners, pennons and flags; by night they are the gongs, drums, reed-pipes and flutes. When the flag waves left, go left; when it waves right, go right. When the drum sounds, advance; when the gong sounds, halt. At one blast, march; at the second, assemble. Whoever does not obey the signal is put to death. When the whole army has submitted to that authority and the soldiers do as they are commanded, then in attack there is no enemy too strong and in assault no formation too firm.” (The <i>fan</i> is a long hanging streamer, the <i>hui</i> the commander’s signal flag; the reed-pipe and flute are wind instruments used to pass orders at night. The marquis asked how to retrieve a confusion; Wu Qi does not answer about this confusion at all, but about the standing signals — by day you look, by night you listen. Ranks come apart because there was no set of marks every man knew. The Sibu congkan text reads “at the <i>second</i> blast, march”; the Siku text gives Wu Qi his surname in the reply.)',
 'p5-2': 'Marquis Wu asked: “If the enemy is many and we are few, what then?” Wu Qi replied: “Avoid him on open ground; waylay him in a defile. Hence the saying: to strike ten with one, nothing is better than a defile; to strike a hundred with ten, nothing is better than broken country; to strike ten thousand with a thousand, nothing is better than an obstacle. Now if a small body of troops rises up suddenly and beats gongs and drums in a narrow pass, then however great the host, none of them will fail to be alarmed. Hence the saying: he who uses many seeks open ground; he who uses few seeks narrows.” (“Defile” and “narrows” render the same word. In “if a small body of troops rises up suddenly,” the first of the two identical graphs is “soldiers” and the second is “suddenly.” The three clauses beginning “nothing is better” turn terrain into a ratio of strengths: use a defile at one to ten, broken country at ten to a hundred, an obstacle at a thousand to ten thousand. The closing sentence is the shortest thing the book says about ground. The base text prints vertical-form colons twice here, which this page has changed. The Sibu congkan text reads “a few <i>young men</i>” for “a small body of troops.”)',
 'p5-3': 'Marquis Wu asked: “There is an army very numerous, warlike and brave; its back is against a great obstacle, mountain on its right and water on its left; it has deep ditches and high ramparts and holds them with strong crossbows; in retreat it moves like a mountain shifting and in attack like wind and rain; and its food is plentiful besides, so that it is hard to hold out against it for long. What then?” Wu Qi replied: “A great question! This is not a matter for the strength of chariots and horsemen; it wants the planning of a sage. Equip a thousand chariots and ten thousand horse, add the foot soldiers to them, and divide into five columns, each column taking a road of its own. With five columns on five roads the enemy will certainly be perplexed and will not know where to put his weight. If the enemy holds fast to make his army secure, send spies quickly to observe what is in his mind. If he listens to what we say, he will raise his works and go; if he does not listen to what we say, behead his envoy and burn his letter, and attack on all five roads. If you win, do not pursue; if you do not win, come back quickly. Then, feigning flight in this way, march unhurriedly and fight fiercely: let one column close on his front and one cut off his rear, while two more, with gags in their mouths, come at him from the right or the left and fall upon his position. With the five columns arriving together you are certain to have the advantage of him. This is the way to strike a strong enemy.” (“A gag” is a short stick held crosswise in the mouth to prevent talking. This is the longest passage in the chapter and the only “planning of a sage” in the book: divide yourself into five so that the enemy cannot tell which road to defend, then give him one chance to negotiate, and only when that fails go in on five roads at once. Negotiation written into a book of tactics is characteristic of the <i>Wuzi</i>; “behead his envoy and burn his letter” says what happens when it fails. The Sibu congkan text drops five characters of the question and miswrites two words in the answer; the Siku text reads “if you do not win, <i>run</i>” for “come back quickly.”)',
 'p5-4': 'Marquis Wu asked: “The enemy is close and bearing down on me; I wish to withdraw and there is no road; my men are very frightened. What then?” Wu Qi replied: “The method for this is as follows. If we are many and they are few, divide and fall upon them. If they are many and we are few, take them with a square formation and keep after them without pause; then however many they are, they can be subdued.” (“Bearing down” is pressing close. “Take them with a square formation” means keeping in square while you move: the square can face all four ways and is the steadiest formation for a force under attack on the march. “Keep after them without pause” is to hold on and give no rest. The question is about having no way out; the answer is eight characters of distinction — if we are many, divide; if we are few, form square. It is the shortest response in the chapter. The Sibu congkan text drops Wu Qi’s name from the reply.)',
 'p5-5': 'Marquis Wu asked: “Suppose we meet the enemy in a ravine, with difficult ground on either hand, and they are many and we are few. What then?” Wu Qi replied: “Wherever you meet hills, wooded valleys, deep mountains or great marshes, go fast and get out; do not take your time. If you come suddenly upon them in a high mountain or a deep valley, you must first beat the drums and raise a shout and fall on them, pushing the bows and crossbows to the front, shooting and taking prisoners as you go. Watch their ordering closely: when it is disordered, strike them and do not hesitate.” (“Get out” is to leave in haste. “Taking prisoners” is seizing men as you advance. The six words “go fast and get out; do not take your time” are the point of the passage: in such country the best response to circumstance is not to be in it. If you truly cannot get away, then take the initiative by noise — “beat the drums and raise a shout” — and by distance, pushing the missile weapons forward. The Siku text reads “watch their <i>government</i>” with a different graph.)',
 'p5-6': 'Marquis Wu asked: “High mountains to right and left, the ground very narrow and cramped; we meet the enemy suddenly, we dare not strike him and we cannot get away. What then?” Wu Qi replied: “This is called fighting in a valley. Numbers are of no use in it. Call out our men of ability, match them against the enemy, and put the light-footed with the sharp weapons in the front rank. Divide the chariots and draw up the horsemen, hidden on all four sides, several <i>li</i> apart, and let none of their troops be seen. The enemy will then hold his ranks firm and dare neither advance nor withdraw. At that point bring out the banners and spread the pennons, march out beyond the mountain and dazzle him, and the enemy will certainly take fright. Provoke him with chariots and horsemen and give him no rest. This is the method of fighting in a valley.” (“Men of ability” are soldiers of physical quality. The <i>pei</i> is the swallow-tail streamer at the end of a flag, here standing for flags generally. “Dazzle him” is to bewilder him — the flags are carried out beyond the mountain so that he will think a further army is there. Both witnesses read “<i>encamp</i> beyond the mountain”; both senses construe and the base reading is kept, with the variant recorded. The whole method lies in letting him see nothing that is real and only what is not: the troops hidden on four sides are invisible and the flags outside the mountain are conspicuous.)',
 'p5-7': 'Marquis Wu asked: “I meet the enemy in a great marsh of standing water; the wheels lean over and the shafts are under water; water is pressing on chariots and horsemen; no boats or oars have been prepared; we can neither advance nor withdraw. What then?” Wu Qi replied: “This is called fighting on water. Do not use chariots or horsemen; leave them to one side for the time being. Climb a height and look about you on all four sides, and you will certainly learn the state of the water: know where it is broad and where narrow, sound it to the bottom for its shallows and its depths, and then you may work a surprise and win by it. If the enemy crosses the water, press him when he is half across.” (“The wheels lean over and the shafts are under water” describes a chariot foundering. “Press him when he is half across” comes from the <i>Sunzi</i>, chapter nine: “when an enemy crosses a river to come at you, do not meet him in the water; let him get half over and then strike.” The order of the answer matters more than the answer: first, chariots and horsemen are no use here; second, find out the water; only then the surprise. Do not be clever before you know the depth. Both witnesses lack the preposition in “meet the enemy <i>in</i> a great marsh,” which the base text has and which reads better; the base text is kept.)',
 'p5-8': 'Marquis Wu asked: “It has rained a long time without stopping; the horses are bogged and the chariots stuck; we are taking the enemy on all four sides and the whole army is in alarm. What then?” Wu Qi replied: “In all use of chariots: in wet and damp, halt; in dry and sunny weather, move. Prefer high ground and shun low; drive your strongest chariots. Whether you advance or halt, keep to the road that will take you. If the enemy gets under way, follow his tracks.” (“Dry and sunny” is fine weather and firm ground. “Prefer high and shun low” is to take the higher ground and keep off the lower. “Follow his tracks” is to pursue along the ruts and hoofprints — after rain, soft ground keeps a record. The question is about being attacked on four sides and the answer talks only about chariots and ground: halt in the wet, move when it dries, keep high. Wu Qi here says nothing about the encirclement and everything about the mud. The Siku text reads “in all use of <i>an army</i>.”)',
 'p5-9': 'Marquis Wu asked: “Violent raiders come out of nowhere, plunder my fields and carry off my cattle and sheep. What then?” Wu Qi replied: “When violent raiders come, you must reckon on their strength; keep a good guard and do not respond. Toward evening they will be leaving, and their loads will be heavy and their hearts uneasy; they will hurry their retreat, and there will certainly be stragglers. Pursue them then and strike, and their force can be destroyed.” (“Violent raiders” are a sudden plundering party. “There will certainly be stragglers” means the column will not hold together. Everything here turns on waiting: do not engage while they are loading; wait until they start home with the load on their backs. The more they have taken, the slower they move and the less steady their nerve. The Siku text reads “carry off my cattle and <i>horses</i>.”)',
 'p5-10': 'Wu Qi said: “The way of attacking an enemy and besieging a city is this. When the walled town has been broken, go each of you into its offices, take charge of its registers of salary and rank, and collect its implements. Wherever the army goes, let no one fell the trees or pull down the houses, take the grain, kill the six domestic animals or burn the stores. Show the people that you have no cruelty in you. Any who ask to surrender, accept and settle them.” (“Take charge of its registers of salary and rank” means receiving them against the books, not looting them. The six animals are the horse, ox, sheep, pig, dog and fowl. “Show the people that you have no cruelty in you” is the whole reason given for the five prohibitions: they are not for the enemy’s sake, they are for the inhabitants to see. A chapter of ten questions and nine answers about changing circumstances ends with something that is not an adjustment at all but a standing prohibition. It corresponds almost clause for clause to the field discipline of <i>Benevolence the Root</i> in the <i>Methods of the Sima</i> — “do not destroy earthworks, do not burn houses, do not fell trees” — and when the Qing editors of the imperial library remarked that the <i>Wuzi</i> “may have taken something from the <i>Methods of the Sima</i>,” this is the place where the resemblance is closest.)',
}
